a tiny audiovisual experience with an overkilled tooling environment.

See current development status at the [project CDash Page](https://my.cdash.org/index.php?project=mole_demo)

## Usage

The software is designed to simply compile and run in as many environments as possible, as long as the minimum development requirements are met. Binary distribution is not available at the moment, but its support would be much wider since most of the dependencies are meant to be self contained.

In order to launch the project, several options are offered:

- .bat file for Windows platforms: just run the script and wait.
- .sh file for Linux platforms: just run the script and wait.
- make file for Linux platforms: just run "make" from the root and wait.
- manual compilation: freely tweak the project at your own will.

In case something unexpected happens you should get a clear error message. In case the project can be partially compiled a full report of success status will be generated. Please contact the developer with the detail in order to improve support.

## Platform support

This version of the project has been tested in the following platforms:

- Debian GNU/Linux 12.0+
- Trisquel GNU/Linux-libre 11.0+
- Microsoft Windows 10+

In order to compile it from source extra packages may be needed. At the time of writing some of them are not available in the usual channels of distribution and may need to be compiled from source including manual resolution of their dependencies.

## Verified toolchains

- CMake 4.0.3+
- Ninja Build 1.11.2 +
- GCC 15.1+

Other toolchains may be possible as long as they support compilation of c++26 and modules, including "import std".
Feel free to send your own CTest reports in order to expand the verified toolchain list.
Alternative versions of the demoscene exist in the port branches, defined by c++ standard (CMake and build system requirements are lowered as a consequence). If you are unable to compile this specific branch, please try with an older one.

## Roadmap

This project started as a college assignment. Once submitted, the scope was extended. Currently development status stays as such:

- [x] Port original Windows project to Linux
- [x] Add multiplatform support for CMake
- [x] Add unit testing
- [x] Add CI dashboard
- [ ] Reach and maintain 75% coverage
- [ ] Create editor
- [ ] Add music tracker
- [ ] Add final project serialization
- [ ] Add scripting
- [ ] Add development hot-reloading support
- [ ] Add end-user interaction support
- [ ] Add packaging and installation
- [ ] Add custom build pipelines
- [ ] Add cross-compilation pipelines
- [ ] Add autogenerated documentation

Development is performed in the latest c++ standard available and implemented at the time. Currently this means a partial version of the c++26 approved draft.

## Desing notes

Demoscene was conceived early on as a modules-only c++20 project. As a consequence, it is not an attempt at modernizing legacy code. The port branches are actually expanding the original modules support with header alternatives, not the other way around.

That said, upon working on the ports I've settled in a project-wide standard consisting on the following:
- Explicitly declare all the export clauses early on.
- Avoid implementation details in module declarations.
Although not required by the modules themselves, this greatly helps maintainability while performing downstream merges to the no-modules branches. I am aware it may be quite unusual given that the average project flow may be retrofitting older code bases instead. However, given that this project is an experiment, I find it much more rewarding to fully take advantage of the current tools and deal later on with the limitations of the past.

Another pillar of the project since the beginning was dependency injection. Given my lack of experience when I started, a runtime polymorphism approach was chosen instead of a static polymorphism one, which would have been a better fit for most of the project needs.

